 has any  experiences with classes that they'd  like to share  please feel free to chime in after  definitely um yeah in addition to lean  launchpad there's so many different  startup classes here at the gsb it seems  like new ones are popping up left and  right and  there's the startup garage which is  obviously also very famous and then  there's launch pad at the design school  there's climate ventures programs and  there's  designing for extreme affordability and  there's so many different types of  entrepreneurship classes that you can  take and so  i guess in addition to classes that help  you i guess incubate your own idea  i also enjoy taking formation of impact  ventures because  um social ventures is also a space that  interests me a lot and  it's basically like a case study class  where you go through  let's say a dozen or so social ventures  over the course of the quarter  um and so yeah i would say it really  depends on what type of knowledge you're  seeking but there has been  a variety of courses that i'm i've been  able to take even as an mba one  i would just close out with saying that  i think the learning also comes from  outside of the classroom and so it's not  just about the course that you're taking  but again  as so many of your classmates have so  much experience and anything and  everything that you could have questions  about  and so just having that really great  advisory board  living on campus with you has been  really helpful  awesome thank you any other favorite  classes that  people are reflecting on  i like strategic communication  and winning writing these are i think  two of my favorite comp space classes  which i think helps really sharpen like  let's say the more intangibles because  of yourself uh in the first class  it's you basically you have to give a uh  a speech in front of class i think it's  once a week and then you watch a video  of yourself and then  you critique yourself uh not  not as not very fun but it i think it is  very very  very helpful in seeing how you come  across to other people and i think  that's something  you know jsp is a very very safe space  to practice these skills and i think  that's  it's really a fantastic platform  i mentioned something a while ago on the  emphasis on soft skills i want to give  examples of that  one of the most impactful classes i took  at the gsb was  touchy feely or formerly known as  interpersonal dynamics  which is basically like the setup is we  were trapped in a room for like forget  four hours  once a week with 11 other people  and all we d all we did were exercises  in which we just gave very straight-up  feedback to each other  and there was emphasis on you know being  vulnerable and authentic  and just being honest with people and in  that setting i was able to communicate  really  you know things i would be uncomfortable  communicating  outside of that context outside of that  safe space  um touching on topics such as race  such as um you know  how one like  how how i how like how how some person  phrased the sentence  was a bit offensive so some other people  like witnessing that and like seeing my  blind spots like that was really  impactful to me and i think  the language and the parlance that that  um  course taught me like are lessons that i  still  bring and i still like use to this day  even in the professional context  especially when you're like  you know having your feedback chat with  your supervisee  at work like super impactful other  classes you can look at  are leadership labs and art of  self-coaching  those two other classes were also super  impactful i won't go into detail but  they're worth looking up really good  classes that are structured around  um paired coaching and um  role playing you get a lot from those  awesome thank you i think you um  actually answered one of the other  questions in the q a box about soft  skills and how  the gsb experience cultivates some of  those soft skills  there's another great question in the q  a box about  leadership so yukiho asks  um how did your leadership change during  the two years at stanford  um in what way and and how  um so vince you mentioned some of the  classes i'm curious for  for other folks how how did your  leadership change  and how did that happen  so to be very honest i wouldn't say that  it's like a  it's like a step jump that you can see  you're at zero one day and you're at one  i think it's a continuous journey  and the point that vince mentioned about  these classes is  that it makes you a lot more self-aware  so it's it's like i can't say that i  am implementing uh everything that i  learned from touchy feely very well but  yes i know that i'm doing much better  than i was probably doing two years back  so  um so yeah there are many things so  that's just one thing i would  sort of caveat the entire answer with  that  you shouldn't think of it like oh these  two years after these two years you'll  be able to actually  do something very drastically different  but what these  courses do is they give you interesting  mental models to think about yourself  be aware of what you're doing what  you're saying how you are behaving with  people  and it just helps you set that context  for really becoming a good leader in the  future  but yeah that's my new sense on this  thanks for that insight that's helpful  um so there's a number of questions  about life  after the gsb as well and  i think a few folks are wondering both  about  sort of the opportunities within the u.s  so perhaps vince you can speak to that  and  other folks are wondering about sort of  transitioning back to  their home country afterwards so what  were your experiences with that  transition  to life after the gsb uh whether you  were looking to return to your home  country or to stay in the u.s  so i could take the staying at the u.s  i might not be able to directly answer  it because  the thing about my scenario was i was  pre-committed  to transfer from the singapore to the  san francisco office even before  starting at the gsb granted  um i think one thing i'd like to point  out  is that for international students the  mba degree at the gsb  has now been accredited as a stem degree  which means that  if you want to stay you are guaranteed  that you can  you're able to work here um for at least  three years post graduation and  in general if you're with you know a  credible company  within those three years um  you should be able to either win a  lottery a  visa lottery that allows you to stay  indefinitely  or you know you could kick start or  finish a green card  process within that time frame and so  like that's one of the things that makes  the transition easier  and also i think one thing i realized  was  the gsb has a disproportionate  representation of people that  stay in one geography after graduation  i think that 50 to 60 percent of  my classmates are still in the bay area  working in various industries and that  just  and as someone who literally like packed  his bags to move here and didn't know  anyone  it's great that the network i actually  built while  i was at the gsb is still within  physical reach  i guess for covid that covet kind of  disrupts that a bit because you can't  i wasn't able to see any people as much  last year but  still like having people within arm's  reach has been helpful  and being able to like you know eat with  people in restaurants readily with a  within with  like a 30 minute uber ride  i'll just add something just i guess  just to put things into perspective  it is obviously more challenging to get  a job in the us as an international  like there's no getting by that it is  true but i guess it's just a comparison  of  you know would it be easier if you  hadn't gone to an mba and hadn't come to  the gsb i would say it's definitely  much advantageous to i guess apply while  you're  in school as an mba candidate and so  it's all about perspective and  i guess if you are committed to staying  in the us  um you could certainly prioritize  employers or types of jobs that  give you sponsorship or pursue an  entrepreneurship journey that could give  you that status and so  it's about your own priorities and so  nothing is guaranteed but there is  certainly a higher chance of you staying  if you choose to pursue that path  and if you want to come back i'm sure  you know i guess that's another question  that i can address which is  uh well kind of um because i'm not an  alumni yet um  we speak about how the alumni pool of  the gsb might be smaller in some  countries and i think that's probably  true  um but i think it also speaks to the the  depth and the strength of the network  because as we've already touched upon  everyone in the network seems to be  super super eager to help out and so  i think it's a it's a double-edged coin  kind of but  um it certainly has a advantage  advantageous  aspects  thank you both for those insights and i  see in the chat will is  also pointing to the bechdel  international center which i don't think  we've talked about yet so i am curious  about some of your experiences working  with the  bechdel international center and how it  supported your transition  from asia to stanford um as is the title  of this event so  any experiences with bechdel or examples  of  of the support and resources they  provide  i think like it they're definitely like  super supportive when it comes to visa  stuff that's all i can say  like anything you you can ask them any  question  about you know the legal the legal  things about staying in the u.s and they  proactively answer  um and you know they  you can walk there and actually ask them  in person pre-pandemic  and they're generally reliable and they  were like just a great resource for me  as someone who was trying to navigate  the  us immigration system as an  international  awesome thank you so i know we were  coming up on time  um i want to take one last question  p